Health Systems, Inc. was founded in 1985 and is headquartered in Franklin, Tennessee.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Kevin J. Hammons CPA | President, CEO & Director | Age 59 | Compensation: $2.7M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 44K people. Revenue per employee of $284K ranks in the below the sector median among Healthcare peers. Each employee generates $5K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $250 per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 10\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (2/10).","financial_summary":["Community Health Systems, Inc. is a micro-cap healthcare business with a $262M market cap on $12.5B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +1% over the past three years and -6% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 6% against a 8% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The business throws off $208M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 2 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.07\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 5.3\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 1.9\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 0.0%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 15% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.0) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":